        "text": "{\"verdict\":\"fail\",\"reasons\":[\"The 'missing' classification is contradicted by the cited text: Section 14.2(a)(iv) is expressly mutual ('Each Party shall use Diligent Efforts to cooperate with the other and/or its designee to effect a smooth and orderly transition ... during the Wind-down Period'), and Article XIV survives under 14.5. A usable transition-assistance obligation running against Array therefore exists, so the comment's statement that 'Array has no obligation to support the transition' misstates the paragraph it is attached to.\",\"Scope creep beyond the playbook position: limb (A) of the insert imposes a new post-termination obligation on Array to 'continue to supply, or secure supply of, Materials in accordance with Article IX'. Neither the preferred/fallback positions nor the model language contemplate a supply-continuity obligation; if Wind-down supply is the real concern it belongs in a supply/survival rule, not bundled into a transition-and-data-return insert. This is not the smallest edit that reaches the playbook position.\",\"Genre/direction mismatch: this is an in-licence in which the licensee (our side) holds and then hands back the licensor's product data by design; there is no vendor-hosted 'Customer Data' to export. The inserted return/deletion mechanic is also expressly subordinated to Array's retained rights under 14.2(a)(iii), (v) and (ix), making it largely self-cancelling and of little negotiating value.\",\"The genuine residual gap - no obligation on Array to return or destroy Ono Confidential Information/Data - is a sub-element short of preferred wording and, on the excerpted (truncated) Article X / 14.5 text, cannot be confirmed as absent document-wide. Under the playbook's classification semantics it should be noted in the rationale (or raised under the confidentiality rule) rather than asserted as a wholly missing transition clause.\"],\"severityAdjustment\":\"low\"}"
